{
  "abstract": "The existence of a directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(88,9,3,0,1)$ remains open. We prove that any such graph has no automorphism subgroup of order twelve or sixteen. Together with the preceding fixed-point and stabilizer theorem, this restricts its full automorphism-group order to $\\{1,2,3,4,6,8\\}$. The argument exhausts nine order-sixteen actions and three order-twelve actions. Four actions force two fixed Kautz graphs whose shared incidence vectors would express a rank-three matrix as a sum with rank at most two. A further action is excluded by exhausting raw incidences on literal forty-eight-vertex partial graphs. For the remaining actions, complete boundary catalogues reduce the graph equations without fixing the outside adjacency. All normalizations are justified by explicit equivariant relabelings. The supplement independently regenerates the action arithmetic, rank calculation, raw incidence catalogues and 202 physical Boolean formulas. Eighty-two formulas have standard-library unit refutations and 120 have independently checked DRAT proofs. Cold reconstructions of the known eighteen- and 108-vertex graphs, including exact complement checks, test the encoder separately from coverage. The proof explicitly depends on the earlier automorphism bound and the standard classification of groups of order sixteen. This substantially restricts intransitive symmetry but permits asymmetric graphs and does not settle the target existence question.",
